#: src/glossary.md:1
msgid "Glossary"
msgstr ""

#: src/glossary.md:3
msgid ""
"Many of the terms used in this guide are common to the Python or Rust "
"ecosystem, and accordingly can be found in the [Python Glossary](https://"
"docs.python.org/3/glossary.html), [Rust Glossary](https://doc.rust-lang.org/"
"reference/glossary.html), or [Cargo Glossary](https://doc.rust-lang.org/"
"cargo/appendix/glossary.html)."
msgstr ""

#: src/glossary.md:5
msgid ""
"Below are some terms that are particularly relevant to PyO3 and either are "
"not found in the above glossaries or have a specific meaning in the context "
"of PyO3:"
msgstr ""

#: src/glossary.md:7
msgid ""
"attached : To call into the Python interpreter from Rust, the Rust thread "
"must have an associated Python [thread state](https://docs.python.org/3/"
"glossary.html#term-thread-state) which is \"attached\" to the Python "
"interpreter. This is a safety invariant required by the Python C API, which "
"ensures that the Python interpreter can handle exceptions, avoid [data races]"
"(https://docs.python.org/3/glossary.html#term-data-race) with the garbage "
"collector, etc. The [`Python::attach`](https://pyo3.rs/main/doc/pyo3/marker/"
"struct.Python.html#method.attach) method is used to attach the current "
"thread to the Python interpreter and obtain a `Python` token which can be "
"used to call Python APIs."
msgstr ""

#: src/glossary.md:12
msgid ""
"extension module : A Python module which is implemeted using native code (e."
"g. C, C++ or Rust) instead of Python. See also [CPython's documentation on "
"creating extension modules](https://docs.python.org/3/extending/extending."
"html)."
msgstr ""

#: src/glossary.md:16
msgid ""
"GIL-enabled Python : The (current, as of Python 3.14) default build of "
"Python, which depends on the [GIL](https://docs.python.org/3/glossary."
"html#term-GIL) for thread safety."
msgstr ""

#: src/glossary.md:19
msgid ""
"    This was historically the only thread safety strategy before the "
"introduction of [free-threaded Python](https://docs.python.org/3/glossary."
"html#term-free-threaded-build).\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: src/glossary.md:21
msgid ""
"Python token : PyO3's [`Python<'py>`](https://pyo3.rs/main/doc/pyo3/marker/"
"struct.Python.html) type, which can only exist when the current thread is "
"[attached](#attached) to the Python interpreter. This type is used to call "
"Python APIs and ensures that the safety invariants required by the Python C "
"API are upheld."
msgstr ""

#: src/glossary.md:25
msgid ""
"smart pointer : Pointers which automatically manage the memory they point "
"to. In particular this guide refers to [PyO3's smart pointers](types."
"md#pyo3s-smart-pointers) `Py<T>`, `Bound<'py, T>`, and `Borrowed<'a, 'py, "
"T>` which point to Python objects and use Python reference counting to "
"ensure correct memory management."
msgstr ""

#: src/glossary.md:29
msgid ""
"    See also the [Rust book's chapter on smart pointers](https://doc.rust-"
"lang.org/book/ch15-00-smart-pointers.html).\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: src/glossary.md:31
msgid ""
"wheel : A precompiled Python package format. Wheels are the standard way to "
"distribute native code in Python packages for common operating systems and "
"Python versions, which avoid the need for users to compile from source. The "
"alternative is known as the \"source distribution\", or \"sdist\", which "
"requires users to compile the code as part of package installation."
msgstr ""
